The key principle is: we decide the architecture now, then optimize inside that architecture rather than repeatedly redesigning it.

Trading Dashboard V2 — Performance & Architecture Requirements
Status: Development baseline / architectural contract
Scope: src/ application and current development implementation
Excluded: ScrapCodes/ is not part of this requirements document unless explicitly brought into scope later.

1. V2 Objective
The primary objective of Trading Dashboard V2 is:

Build a fast, low-RAM, multi-chart trading dashboard while preserving all existing V1 functionality.

Performance optimization must prioritize:

Crosshair responsiveness
Data loading
Timeframe processing
RAM usage
Python ↔ JavaScript communication
Chart rendering
Drawing performance
An optimization is not considered successful if it significantly increases RAM usage merely to improve CPU or latency.

2. Architectural Decision — FINAL DIRECTION
V2 will retain the existing fundamental architecture:

Python / PySide6
        │
        ├── Application / UI
        │
        ├── Market Data Layer
        │
        └── Chart Manager
                 │
                 ├── Chart Instance 1
                 ├── Chart Instance 2
                 ├── Chart Instance 3
                 └── ...
                         │
                  QWebEngineView
                         │
                    QWebChannel
                         │
                  JavaScript Chart
                         │
                 Lightweight Charts

We will not replace Qt/QWebEngine/Lightweight Charts with another framework during V2.

We will optimize the existing architecture.

3. Chart Architecture
Each visible chart remains an independent chart slot.

A chart slot owns:

instrument
timeframe
chart state
candle data required by the chart
Lightweight Charts instance
QWebEngineView
JavaScript bridge
The application must support the existing layouts, including:

1 chart
2 charts
3 charts
4 charts
5 charts
6 charts
8 charts
The architecture must therefore be designed and tested for 8 simultaneous charts.

4. Chart Lifecycle
We will separate:

Chart existence
Whether a chart/WebEngine instance exists.

Chart visibility
Whether that chart is currently visible in the selected layout.

Changing layouts should not unnecessarily recreate the entire application architecture.

However, V2 must investigate whether keeping unused QWebEngine instances alive creates excessive RAM usage.

The final lifecycle strategy will be chosen based on measured RAM usage.

The following principle is mandatory:

Do not destroy/recreate chart engines simply as an optimization unless benchmarking proves the lifecycle change is beneficial overall.

5. Market Data Architecture
The market-data layer will become a distinct responsibility.

Conceptually:

Data Source
    ↓
Data Manager
    ↓
Raw OHLC data
    ↓
Timeframe Engine
    ↓
Processed timeframe data
    ↓
Chart Manager
    ↓
JavaScript

The chart layer must not independently perform expensive market-data loading whenever possible.

6. Excel Loading
Current behavior loads Excel sheets through pandas.

V2 will introduce a controlled data-loading strategy.

Requirements:

Avoid unnecessary repeated pd.read_excel() operations.
Avoid loading the same sheet repeatedly when data is already available.
Do not blindly cache every sheet forever.
RAM usage must be monitored.
Cache eviction/lifecycle must be deterministic if caching is introduced.
Raw data should have a single authoritative representation where practical.
The objective is:

Repeated request
      ↓
Already loaded?
      ├── YES → reuse
      └── NO  → load once

But caching must remain RAM-conscious.

7. Raw Data Ownership
V2 should establish one authoritative raw-data representation per instrument/data source.

We must avoid unnecessary duplication such as:

DataFrame
   ↓
copy
   ↓
list[dict]
   ↓
JSON string
   ↓
JavaScript objects
   ↓
Lightweight Charts internal data

The same market data should not exist in multiple Python structures unless there is a measurable reason.

Any additional representation must have a documented purpose.

8. Timeframe Engine
Timeframe processing becomes a dedicated optimization target.

Supported existing timeframe behavior must remain unchanged.

The engine must:

preserve OHLC correctness
preserve session boundaries
preserve timestamps
preserve daily aggregation
preserve intraday aggregation
preserve chart output
The engine should avoid:

unnecessary DataFrame copies
unnecessary sorting
repeated conversions
repeated aggregation of unchanged data
Where practical:

Raw data
    ↓
Timeframe result
    ↓
Cache/reuse

A timeframe should not be recalculated unnecessarily when the underlying source data has not changed.

9. Crosshair Architecture
Crosshair synchronization remains timestamp-based.

This is a fixed architectural requirement.

Source chart
    ↓
Mouse X position
    ↓
Source market timestamp
    ↓
Synchronization manager
    ↓
Destination charts
    ↓
Nearest candle for that timestamp
    ↓
Destination candle's own price

The source chart's price must never be used as the destination chart's price.

This behavior must remain unchanged.

10. Crosshair Lookup
The current destination lookup performs a linear search through candle data.

V2 will replace this with an efficient indexed lookup.

Target complexity:

Current:
O(N)

Target:
O(log N)

or better where practical.

The preferred implementation is a timestamp-indexed/binary-search strategy.

Requirements:

preserve nearest-candle behavior
preserve missing-time handling
preserve null coordinate protection
preserve crosshair visual behavior
support different instruments with different trading timestamps
No full-array scan should occur on every crosshair synchronization event unless explicitly justified.

11. Crosshair Event Rate
Mouse movement can generate a very high number of events.

V2 must prevent unnecessary work.

The synchronization system should:

avoid processing identical timestamps repeatedly
avoid unnecessary Python ↔ JavaScript calls
avoid synchronizing invisible charts
avoid unnecessary destination updates
avoid redundant crosshair positioning
Conceptually:

Mouse movement
      ↓
Determine timestamp
      ↓
Same timestamp as previous?
      ├── YES → skip
      └── NO  → synchronize

Any throttling/debouncing must preserve perceived real-time responsiveness.

12. Python ↔ JavaScript Communication
QWebChannel remains the communication architecture.

However, communication volume must be minimized.

V2 should avoid:

mouse event
    ↓
Python
    ↓
JavaScript
    ↓
Python
    ↓
JavaScript

when the same result can be achieved with fewer crossings.

Communication responsibilities should be clearly separated:

Python
Responsible for:

application state
instrument selection
timeframe selection
data management
chart coordination
JavaScript
Responsible for:

chart rendering
mouse/chart coordinates
crosshair rendering
drawing
browser-side chart operations
The boundary should be optimized rather than removed.

13. JavaScript Candle Data
The JavaScript side must avoid unnecessary copies of candle data.

Current structures such as:

currentCandleData
currentDataTimes
Lightweight Charts internal data

must be evaluated for duplication.

V2 should retain only the structures necessary for:

chart rendering
timestamp lookup
crosshair synchronization
drawing/interaction
If a timestamp index is introduced, it must be memory-efficient.

14. Chart Rendering
Chart rendering must remain visually equivalent to V1.

V2 should investigate:

unnecessary fitContent() calls
unnecessary autoscaling
duplicate redraws
redundant applyOptions() calls
repeated chart initialization
unnecessary updates when data/state has not changed
A chart should update only what actually changed.

15. Full Data Reload vs Incremental Update
V2 should distinguish between:

Full reload
Used when:

instrument changes
timeframe changes
complete dataset changes
Incremental update
Used when only a small amount of new data changes.

The architecture should support incremental updates where the data source allows it.

However, incremental updates must not be introduced merely for complexity's sake if the current source is static Excel data.

The implementation must match the actual data-source requirements.

16. Memory Requirements
RAM is a first-class performance metric.

Testing must include:

1 chart
2 charts
4 charts
6 charts
8 charts
popup windows where applicable
For each scenario measure:

startup RAM
post-load RAM
peak RAM
RAM after timeframe changes
RAM after symbol changes
RAM after repeated refreshes
RAM after opening/closing popup charts
A repeated operation must not continuously increase RAM.

Particular attention must be paid to:

Python objects
DataFrames
JSON strings
QWebEngine processes
JavaScript arrays
Lightweight Charts data
cached datasets

17. No Memory Leaks
V2 must specifically test for:

Load chart
    ↓
Change symbol
    ↓
Change timeframe
    ↓
Repeat

and:

Open popup
    ↓
Close popup
    ↓
Repeat

Memory must eventually stabilize.

A performance optimization that causes memory growth is rejected.

18. Performance Instrumentation
Before major optimization, V2 must establish a baseline.

Instrumentation should measure:

Data
Excel read duration
DataFrame preparation duration
timeframe aggregation duration
candle serialization duration
JavaScript data-transfer duration
Crosshair
mouse event frequency
timestamp lookup duration
Python ↔ JS round-trip duration
destination lookup duration
total synchronization duration
Memory
process RSS
peak memory
memory before/after major operations
Rendering
chart initialization duration
data-set duration
layout-change duration
Instrumentation must be lightweight and removable/disableable.

19. Optimization Method
Every major optimization follows this exact process:

1. Establish baseline
        ↓
2. Identify bottleneck
        ↓
3. Form hypothesis
        ↓
4. Make ONE controlled change
        ↓
5. Test functionality
        ↓
6. Measure performance
        ↓
7. Measure RAM
        ↓
8. Compare against baseline
        ↓
9. Keep or revert

We do not combine five architectural changes and then attempt to determine which one helped.

20. Regression Requirements
Every optimization must preserve:

chart loading
instrument switching
timeframe switching
chart layouts
8-chart operation
crosshair synchronization
timestamp alignment
destination-specific prices
drawing functionality
popup functionality
existing UI behavior
Performance improvements that break existing functionality are rejected.

21. Architecture Changes
The following are not allowed during normal optimization:

replacing PySide6/Qt
replacing QWebEngine
replacing Lightweight Charts
replacing the entire Python/JavaScript communication model
rewriting the application from scratch
moving the whole application to another framework
Such changes require a separate architectural review.

The goal is to make the current architecture efficient, not repeatedly replace it.

22. Code Organization Direction
The current large main.py should eventually be decomposed only when there is a clear responsibility boundary.

The target conceptual modules are:

Application
    │
    ├── Layout Manager
    │
    ├── Chart Manager
    │       └── Chart Slot
    │
    ├── Data Manager
    │       ├── Raw Data
    │       └── Cache
    │
    ├── Timeframe Engine
    │
    ├── Crosshair Manager
    │
    └── Communication Layer

However:

Do not perform a large refactor solely to make the code look cleaner.

Refactoring should happen when it enables a performance improvement, testability, ownership boundary, or prevents architectural duplication.

23. V2 Development Phases
Phase 0 — Architecture Freeze
Document and preserve the architecture defined in this document.

No major rewrite.

Phase 1 — Performance Baseline
Build measurement instrumentation.

Measure:

startup
data loading
timeframe switching
symbol switching
crosshair
1/4/8 chart RAM
repeated operations
Deliverable: baseline performance report.

Phase 2 — Crosshair Engine
Optimize:

event frequency
timestamp lookup
destination lookup
redundant synchronization
Python ↔ JS traffic
Target:

O(N) lookup → O(log N) or better

Deliverable: faster crosshair with identical behavior.

Phase 3 — Data Loading
Optimize:

Excel reads
repeated sheet loading
raw-data ownership
controlled caching
Deliverable: faster symbol/data loading without uncontrolled RAM growth.

Phase 4 — Timeframe Engine
Optimize:

copies
sorting
aggregation
repeated calculations
timeframe caching/reuse
Deliverable: faster timeframe changes with identical OHLC results.

Phase 5 — Data Transfer
Optimize:

Python
   ↓
serialization
   ↓
QWebEngine
   ↓
JavaScript

Reduce unnecessary data copies and serialization overhead.

Deliverable: lower transfer latency and lower peak memory.

Phase 6 — Chart Rendering
Optimize:

fitContent
autoscaling
redraws
chart initialization
unnecessary updates
Deliverable: smoother chart updates.

Phase 7 — RAM Optimization
Analyze:

QWebEngine instances
JavaScript data
cached data
DataFrames
popup lifecycle
hidden chart lifecycle
Deliverable: stable RAM behavior under 8-chart workloads and repeated operations.

Phase 8 — Drawing Performance
Only after the core data/crosshair/RAM architecture is stable.

Optimize:

drawing operations
canvas updates
mouse interaction
redraw frequency
object management
Existing drawing behavior must remain unchanged.

Phase 9 — Final Regression & V2 Freeze
Run the complete test matrix.

Test:

1 chart
2 charts
4 charts
6 charts
8 charts
+
popup
+
symbol changes
+
timeframe changes
+
crosshair
+
drawing
+
repeated operations

Once the performance targets are met:

V2 architecture is frozen.

Future feature development must work within this architecture unless a separate architecture-change decision is made.

